sections.s2_technical:
WHY inheritance can be problematic — the FRAGILE BASE CLASS problem:

```java
// ═══ FRAGILE BASE CLASS: parent change breaks child ═══
public class Account {
    protected BigDecimal balance = BigDecimal.ZERO;

    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) {
        balance = balance.add(amount);
    }

    // Later, Account adds this "helpful" method:
    public void depositAll(List<BigDecimal> amounts) {
        for (BigDecimal amount : amounts) {
            deposit(amount);  // calls deposit() for each
        }
    }
}

public class AuditedAccount extends Account {
    private int transactionCount = 0;

    @Override
    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) {
        super.deposit(amount);
        transactionCount++;  // count each deposit
    }
}

// BUG: depositAll([100, 200, 300]) → transactionCount = 3 (correct!)
// But what if Account changes depositAll() to NOT call deposit()?
// Or what if depositAll() calls deposit() internally AND the child's deposit increments count?
// The child's behavior DEPENDS on the parent's implementation details — FRAGILE!
```

COMPOSITION solves this — no inheritance dependency:

```java
// ═══ COMPOSITION: HAS-A instead of IS-A ═══
public class AuditedAccount {
    private final Account account;  // HAS-A Account (delegation)
    private int transactionCount = 0;

    public AuditedAccount(Account account) {
        this.account = account;
    }

    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) {
        account.deposit(amount);  // DELEGATES to Account
        transactionCount++;        // adds OUR logic
    }

    public void depositAll(List<BigDecimal> amounts) {
        for (BigDecimal amount : amounts) {
            this.deposit(amount);  // calls OUR deposit — count is correct!
        }
        // We control the flow. Parent changes don't break us.
    }

    public BigDecimal getBalance() { return account.getBalance(); }
    public int getTransactionCount() { return transactionCount; }
}
// AuditedAccount doesn't EXTEND Account — it WRAPS it.
// Account can change internally — AuditedAccount is SAFE.
```

FOUR reasons to PREFER composition:

```java
// REASON 1: FLEXIBILITY — swap implementations at runtime
public class TradeService {
    private FeeCalculator feeCalculator;  // HAS-A — injected!

    public TradeService(FeeCalculator feeCalculator) {
        this.feeCalculator = feeCalculator;
    }

    public BigDecimal executeTrade(BigDecimal amount) {
        BigDecimal fee = feeCalculator.calculate(amount);
        return amount.add(fee);
    }
}
// In production: new TradeService(new StandardFeeCalculator())
// In testing: new TradeService(new ZeroFeeCalculator())
// In promotion: new TradeService(new DiscountedFeeCalculator())
// SWAP at runtime — impossible with inheritance!

// REASON 2: MULTIPLE REUSE — has-a many things
public class TradeService {
    private final FeeCalculator feeCalc;         // has-a
    private final NotificationService notifier;  // has-a
    private final AuditService auditor;          // has-a
    private final ComplianceChecker compliance;  // has-a
    // Can use 4 different capabilities. With inheritance: only extends ONE class!
}

// REASON 3: AVOIDS FRAGILE BASE CLASS — no parent dependency
// Parent changes don't break children. Composition delegates — doesn't inherit internals.

// REASON 4: ENCAPSULATION — doesn't expose parent's methods
// Inheritance: child exposes ALL parent's public methods (even unwanted ones!)
// Composition: child exposes ONLY what it chooses to delegate.
```

WHEN to use inheritance (it's not always wrong!):

```java
// USE INHERITANCE when:
// 1. TRUE IS-A relationship (SavingsAccount IS-A Account — business domain truth)
// 2. Need SHARED MUTABLE STATE (balance field shared across account types)
// 3. Template Method Pattern (parent defines algorithm, child fills steps)
// 4. Framework requirement (extends HttpServlet, extends AbstractController)

// USE COMPOSITION when:
// 1. HAS-A relationship (TradeService HAS-A FeeCalculator)
// 2. Need to SWAP behavior at runtime (Strategy pattern)
// 3. Need MULTIPLE capabilities (has fee + notification + audit + compliance)
// 4. Want to CONTROL what's exposed (hide parent's unwanted methods)
// 5. DEFAULT choice — when in doubt, compose!
```

